# general-knowledge-time.kb - General Knowledge / Time
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 5 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
#
# The current time cannot come from a written bank, and the entry for
# it says so. It is the single most common question a person asks a
# chatbot and the one most likely to produce a confident wrong answer,
# so it is worth stating the limit clearly.

time | definition | Time is the ordering of events, and the ordinary question of what time it is needs a live clock, which this bank does not have. I can explain how time is measured, why time zones exist, and how to read any clock you are given, but I will not invent a reading. If you need the time, look at a device; it is faster than anything I could say and it is right. | time, oras, anong oras, ngayon, karon, clock, live, zone, measure | what time is it, anong oras na, what is the time now, karon anong oras | time | oras
time zone | definition | A time zone is a region whose clocks agree, and the offsets from Greenwich range from about minus twelve to plus fourteen, which is why it is nearly always wrong to guess. The reason the offsets are not whole hours is that they are political: a zone boundary is usually a border, and when one moves its clocks the neighbours have to agree. The Philippines is eight hours ahead of Greenwich and does not use daylight saving, which is a useful fact for anyone catching up with a call. | time zone, offset, greenwich, utc, daylight, border | what is a time zone, what is gmt, is the philippines 8 hours ahead | time zone | timezone
daylight saving time | definition | Daylight saving time is the practice of moving clocks forward in summer so that evenings are lighter, and the stated reason is saving energy on lighting. The evidence for the energy saving is weak, and the real effects are a lost hour in spring and a repeated hour in autumn. The Philippines does not observe it, and neither do most of Asia, which leaves the country permanently out of step with Europe and North America for half the year. | daylight saving, summer, clock, energy, spring, autumn | what is daylight saving time, does the philippines use daylight saving, why do clocks change | daylight saving time | daylight saving
calendar | definition | A calendar is a system for dividing time into countable units, and the one in use is built from astronomy that was settled long before it. Months come from lunar cycles, which is why their lengths are irregular, while the week is seven days for a historical reason that has no astronomical support at all. The awkward parts are inherited: leap years, months with thirty days, and a year that does not divide evenly into anything. | calendar, month, week, lunar, irregular, kalendaryo, system, dividing, countable, units | why is a month 30 days, why are there 7 days in a week, what is a calendar | kalendaryo | calendar
timetable | definition | A timetable is a published schedule of when something happens, and the word is most used for transport where the times are fixed and the service runs whether or not you need it. It is a different object from a calendar, which organises your own time, and from a schedule, which is a plan somebody arranges. Reading one properly means looking for the change times rather than the departure times, since those are the ones that catch people out. | timetable, schedule, departure, transport, change, published | what is a timetable, difference between timetable and schedule, how to read a train timetable | timetable | iskedyed
leap year | definition | A leap year is a year with an extra day, 366 instead of 365, and the rule exists because the Earth does not take a whole number of days to orbit the Sun. The correction is not arbitrary: a tropical year is about 365.2425 days, so adding a full day every four years over-corrects by a little, and to match the fraction instead of the whole, years divisible by four are leap years except those divisible by one hundred, unless they are also divisible by four hundred. So 2000 was a leap year and 1900 was not, which is the detail that makes the rule memorable and the detail people most often get wrong. The whole system collapses every 3,200 years, which is negligible. The result is that the calendar drifts against the seasons by less than a day across its whole history, and a leap year is what keeps the date of the equinox roughly fixed. In the Philippines the practical consequences are administrative rather than astronomical: a birth date falling on 29 February only comes around every four years, and a contract or a term counted in years can be one day longer. | leap, february, 366, century, calendar, orbit, drift, equinox | what is a leap year, why is 1900 not a leap year, when is the next leap year | bisan | leap year

